    "seo": {
      "title": "Virtual Tours of Iranian Heritage | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Understand reconstructed Persepolis and online exhibitions about Iranian archaeology, with clear distinctions between evidence and interpretation."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "different-kinds-of-tour",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What kind of place are you entering?",
        "content": "A virtual tour may reconstruct a vanished building or present an exhibition assembled around surviving objects. These formats answer different questions. Getty’s Persepolis Reimagined offers an interpreted ancient setting, while the Death By Salt website introduces a digitized exhibition about Iranian archaeology. Neither should automatically be read as a current visual survey of an Iranian destination. [s1][s2]\n\nFor readers unfamiliar with Iran, this distinction helps connect digital experiences with physical geography. Persepolis is an archaeological site whose monumental remains are recognized by UNESCO. A reconstruction can help explain those remains, but its completed spaces should be understood alongside evidence from the surviving site. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "persepolis-reimagined",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Persepolis as an interpreted ancient environment",
        "content": "Getty announced Persepolis Reimagined in April 2022. Its described route begins at the Gate of All Nations, then explores major palace spaces, including the Apadana reception hall and Royal Treasury. Interpretation connects architecture with objects and court practices; the experience also incorporates present-day views. Getty credits collaboration among museum specialists, university consultants, and digital production teams. [s1]\n\nUNESCO describes the actual monument as a palace complex built on a terrace combining natural and constructed ground, with architectural inspiration from Mesopotamia. Reading that account alongside a reconstruction helps separate the archaeological setting from the visual choices used to make an ancient environment legible. A beautifully finished digital room is an explanation to examine, rather than proof that every depicted detail is known. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "exhibition-tours",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Following an exhibition rather than rebuilding a city",
        "content": "Death By Salt provides a contrasting approach. Its website introduces digital exhibition sections about the Chehrābād mine, research across the Iranian Highlands, and a collaborative exhibition on the search for resources. Here, the organizing framework is an exhibition narrative rather than a reconstructed urban landscape. [s2]\n\nThis format invites a different reading habit: identify the institutions responsible for the interpretation, follow the relationship between objects and explanatory material, and distinguish the exhibition’s historical presentation from present visitor information. The site contains legacy pandemic guidance, so its practical notices should not be treated as freshly confirmed arrangements. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "using-tours-well",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A companion to further exploration",
        "content": "These examples suggest a useful sequence: establish what survives, examine how the digital presentation interprets it, and return to the source institution for context. Getty’s published description and UNESCO’s site account provide complementary starting points for Persepolis. [s1][s3]\n\nThe sources inspected here document the projects and their intended content. They do not establish uninterrupted playback, accessibility, or compatibility on every device. A virtual experience can deepen an encounter with Iranian heritage while still leaving practical travel questions—entry, opening arrangements, and current physical access—to separate verification.",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3"]
      }
    ],
