    "description": "Attraction discovery platforms organize destinations through searches, categories, and maps. Visit Iran’s tourism interface and UNESCO’s property records illustrate complementary ways to discover places while checking their identity and heritage significance.",
    "tags": ["Attraction discovery", "Travel research", "Visit Iran", "UNESCO", "Destination information"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T23:02:57Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T23:02:57Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Attraction Discovery Platforms for Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Learn how tourism categories and authoritative heritage records can help identify Iranian attractions without confusing discovery with verified access."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "from-interest-to-place",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Turning an interest into a destination",
        "content": "Discovery begins with a question: a historic building, a landscape, a museum, or a place associated with a cultural tradition. Visit Iran’s English tourism map presents named search and category filters, giving readers several ways to begin. Its visible interface separates attractions from facilities and infrastructure, a useful distinction between the reason for a visit and the services supporting it. [s1]\n\nThe existence of a filter establishes how the interface organizes information. It does not establish how complete its records are, whether every result is accurate, or whether a listed place can currently receive visitors. Discovery should therefore lead to further checking rather than end the research process.",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "categories-and-coverage",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What a tourism interface makes visible",
        "content": "Visit Iran offers historical categories such as museums, bridges, bazaars, houses, and palaces, alongside natural features such as caves, mountains, and wetlands or other landscape categories. It also distinguishes cities and rural destinations and provides filters associated with World Heritage and craft cities. These groupings let a reader move beyond a single famous monument toward a wider set of interests. [s1]\n\nSuch categories are an editorial structure, not a ranking of importance. A place appearing under one heading may also matter for another reason. Nor should a facility listing be mistaken for a confirmed reservation or a complete account of accessibility. The inspected interface supports discovery; its underlying results and operational details were not independently audited. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "checking-the-entity",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Check whether a name means one place or several",
        "content": "Authoritative heritage records can clarify what a search result represents. UNESCO’s Persian Garden entry concerns nine gardens in different provinces, rather than one destination called the Persian Garden. Its description explains shared design principles while recognizing adaptation to different climates. A single heritage label can therefore connect geographically separate places. [s2]\n\nPersepolis provides a different case: UNESCO’s record describes a particular archaeological palace complex and its terrace setting. Comparing these records shows why the underlying entity matters. A monument, a group of components, and a broad cultural category should not be treated as interchangeable map points. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "discovery-to-verification",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Build a shortlist, then verify it",
        "content": "A practical research method is to use broad categories to discover candidates, then read a more specific record to understand the site’s identity and significance. For a garden-related itinerary, the UNESCO description helps explain why related places may share a designation without sharing a location. For Persepolis, the property record establishes the archaeological subject behind the name. [s2][s3]\n\nNeither step alone confirms opening arrangements, transport, ticketing, or present conditions. Those details require separately dated information from the relevant operator or authority. Keeping discovery and practical verification as distinct stages makes a shortlist more useful: each place has both an understood identity and a clear list of travel details still to check.",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      }
    ],
